{# regulated_verifier - stage 2 of the regulated screen (design 9.6).

   app/pipeline/defect_check.py:166-175 inverted: there the literal word
   REFUTED clears a defect claim and anything else keeps it. Here the
   literal word not_licensed clears a regulated claim and anything else -
   a synonym, a hedge, a paragraph, a provider error - keeps the matter
   regulated and routed. Under-routing is the dangerous direction; a
   verifier that could clear a claim with prose would be a single-stage
   judge with extra steps. #}
You are an adversarial verifier. A first-stage screen has claimed that a candidate in a consulting engagement touches a regulated domain: a matter where the correct answer depends on a licence, a statute, a regulator, a court, a contract's enforceability or a professional duty. Your job is to try to REFUTE the claim - to show the matter is ordinary business judgement a consultant may answer.

THE CLAIM
- candidate id: {{ claim.entity_id }}
- candidate kind: {{ claim.kind }}
- candidate wording: {{ claim.text }}
- claimed domain: {{ claim.domain }} (one of {{ RegulatedDomain | values }})
- trigger: {{ claim.trigger }}
- stage-1 reason: {{ claim.reason }}

CONTEXT (id, kind, wording) - the only entities you may cite
{% for e in entities %}
- {{ e.id }} [{{ e.kind }}]: {{ e.text }}
{% endfor %}

VERDICT - one word decides, and only one word clears:
- not_licensed : the matter can be answered fully by a consultant without any licence, statute, regulator, contract interpretation or professional duty entering the answer. Return this ONLY when you are certain.
- licensed : the claim stands. Return this whenever you are uncertain, whenever the answer would depend on a jurisdiction, and whenever a professional would be expected to sign off.

RULES
- Uncertainty is not a refutation. If you would need to add "it depends" or "in most cases", the verdict is licensed.
- Cite entity ids. The reason names the context entities it relies on.
- Never invent a number. The reason states no fine, threshold, rate or deadline.
- An empty list is a real answer. "cited_ids" may be empty when the verdict rests on the candidate wording alone.

The <angle-bracket> notation in the JSON shape below is schema documentation, NEVER output format - no value in your output is wrapped in < >.

Return ONLY this JSON:
{
  "entity_id": "{{ claim.entity_id }}",
  "verdict": "<not_licensed | licensed>",
  "reason": "<one sentence>",
  "cited_ids": ["<entity id>"]
}
